1
00:00:03,250 --> 00:00:06,883
Image Tool resizes, converts, and compresses pictures,

2
00:00:06,933 --> 00:00:08,602
right on your desktop.

3
00:00:08,652 --> 00:00:12,470
Let's try it on a photo that's a little too big to share.

4
00:00:13,572 --> 00:00:17,310
I'll choose the image. The app reads it straight away,

5
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:20,720
and shows its size, its type, and its file size.

6
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:24,192
This one's nineteen twenty by ten eighty, a JPEG,

7
00:00:24,242 --> 00:00:26,402
around six hundred kilobytes.

8
00:00:27,504 --> 00:00:31,888
First, a resize. I'll go by width, and type eight hundred.

9
00:00:31,938 --> 00:00:36,253
Then I'll switch the image type to WEBP, and hit Process.

10
00:00:36,303 --> 00:00:39,925
And there it is: a preview, the new dimensions,

11
00:00:39,975 --> 00:00:42,004
and a much smaller file.

12
00:00:43,115 --> 00:00:46,995
Now let's try compressing. I'll keep the size as it is,

13
00:00:47,045 --> 00:00:51,377
turn on Compress, and set the quality to sixty. Process again,

14
00:00:51,427 --> 00:00:55,565
and we get a JPEG about forty percent of the original size.

15
00:00:57,176 --> 00:00:58,777
Finally, Show in folder.

16
00:00:58,827 --> 00:01:02,024
All the results are saved right next to the original,

17
00:01:02,074 --> 00:01:05,986
which is never changed, so you can compare the sizes side by side.

18
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:12,214
That's Image Tool. Resize, convert, and compress, in a few clicks.

19
00:01:12,264 --> 00:01:14,990
Try it on one of your own images.
